  "narrative": {
    "intro": "District of Columbia has $1B in federal contract obligations for FY2027 to date. District of Columbia recorded 3,573 awards this fiscal year to date and the average award size is $282K. District of Columbia obligations fell 97.0% year over year. District of Columbia obligations by fiscal year show a large prior scale: FY22 was $31.6B, FY23 was $34B, FY24 was $36B, FY25 was $31.5B, and FY26 was $33.4B. District of Columbia spending in FY27 to date is focused on technology and services, with Computer Systems Design Services at $178M and Other Computer Related Services at $119M among the top industries. District of Columbia top buying agencies so far are the Department of Transportation with $229M and the General Services Administration with $180M. District of Columbia top vendors so far include HARRIS CORPORATION at $56.3M and CROWLEY GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. at $40.9M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are the biggest buyers in District of Columbia this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Transportation is the largest buyer with $229M in obligations. The General Services Administration follows with $180M, and the Department of Homeland Security follows with $107M. The Department of Veterans Affairs, the Department of Energy, and the Department of the Treasury are also significant buyers at $89.8M, $67.3M, and $60.7M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate federal contracting in District of Columbia right now?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry at $178M, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $119M and Engineering Services at $90.3M. HARRIS CORPORATION and CROWLEY GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. are the top two vendors at $56.3M and $40.9M respectively, with CGI FEDERAL INC. and ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC also among the leaders."
      }
